<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Barcelona</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/878">878</a>–<a href="/years/1410">1410</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> was the dynasty of counts of Barcelona descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilfred-the-hairy">Wilfred the Hairy</a>, who gathered several Catalan counties under one line in the late 9th century. By the marriage of Ramon Berenguer IV and Petronilla in 1137 it acquired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon</a> and ruled the Crown of Aragon until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-of-aragon">Martin the Humane</a> died in 1410.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The house descended from the Bellonids, the descendants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilfred-the-hairy">Wilfred the Hairy</a>, and held the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> and the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girona">Girona</a> continuously from 878. By the thirteenth century its members had inherited most of the Catalan counties and had established a territorial Principality of Catalonia. Ausona and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/besalu">Besalú</a> joined the family's holdings in 1111, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berga">Berga</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerdanya">Cerdagne</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conflent">Conflent</a> in 1118, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roussillon">Roussillon</a> in 1172 and Pallars Jussà in 1192.</p><p>The union with Aragon brought the title of prince of Aragon in 1137; the title of king of Aragon followed in 1162. Further crowns came through conquest and inheritance: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> in 1238, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Majorca</a> in 1276, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> in 1282, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a> in 1297. The family also held the lordship of Montpellier from 1204 to 1249, and members of the line were dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> from 1312 to 1388 and of Neopatria from 1319 to 1390.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The family divided into several lines. Cadet branches ruled Urgell from 992 and Gandia, and others had ruled Ausona intermittently from 878 until 1111. A cadet branch held Provence from 1112 to 1245 and another ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> from 1282 to 1409. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/besalu">Besalú</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerdanya">Cerdagne</a> came to the main line by inheritance from another branch of the family, and the family held the title of count of Urgell again from 1314 to 1413.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The main line ended with the death of its last legitimate male member, Martin, in 1410. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/compromise-of-caspe">Compromise of Caspe</a> of 1412 the Crown of Aragon passed to a branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-trastamara">House of Trastámara</a>, descended from the infanta Eleanor of the house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>, so that the inheritance went through a woman of the old line.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “House of Barcelona” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its counts were often in conflict with the counts of Barcelona and the bishops of Girona, while remaining vassals of the <b>House of Barcelona</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-empuries">County of Empúries</a></li><li><blockquote>Osona remained in the hands of the <b>House of Barcelona</b>, briefly granted to younger sons, until it was fully merged with the County of Barcelona.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-osona">County of Osona</a></li><li><blockquote>They became vassals of the <b>Counts of Barcelona</b> and later the Crown of Aragon, but kept wide local powers.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-pallars-sobira">County of Pallars Sobirà</a></li><li><blockquote>Later the county passed to a branch of the royal <b>House of Barcelona</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-urgell">County of Urgell</a></li><li><blockquote>Its rulers, all freedmen, lacked dynastic legitimacy and leaned on stronger neighbours such as Dénia and Zaragoza, and on payments to the <b>Counts of Barcelona</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-tortosa">Taifa of Tortosa</a></li></ul></section>
